BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jewish Joy\, Imperial Power\,and the Rabbis of Roman Palestine
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260929T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260929T183000
DTSTAMP:20260920T135220Z
UID:dartmouth-82776@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=82776\n\nDescription:\nJewish history under Roman rule has long b
 een told as\n“a history of suffering.” From this perspective\, joy is 
 considered incongruous with the experience of the rabbis of Roman Palestin
 e. This talk puts joy back in rabbinic history. It is not\, however\, a fe
 el-good lecture (at least not only). Instead\, the talk will explore what 
 rabbinic literature reveals about the nexus between public joy and imperia
 l power in Palestine\, elsewhere in the Roman world\, and even within asym
 metric political systems of other times and places.\n\nSponsor / organizer
 : History Department\, Jewish Studies Program\n\nAudience: Public\n\nSourc
 e: Dartmouth\n\nWhy this was included: Free Food category\n\nFood availabi
 lity is inferred from the source listing\; verify details before attending
 .
LAST-MODIFIED:20260920T135220Z
LOCATION:Carson Hall L01
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
